  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2509.08614v1 [eess.SP] 10 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuxuan Duan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Chenyang Yang
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent trends in learning wireless policies attempt to develop deep neural networks (DNNs) for handling multiple tasks with a single model. Existing approaches often rely on large models, which are hard to pre-train and fine-tune at the wireless edge. In this work, we challenge this paradigm by leveraging the structured knowledge of wireless problems – specifically, permutation equivariant (PE) properties. We design three types of PE-aware modules, two of which are Transformer-style sub-layers. These modules can serve as building blocks to assemble compact DNNs applicable to the wireless policies with various PE properties. To guide the design, we analyze the hypothesis space associated with each PE property, and show that the PE-structured module assembly can boost the learning efficiency.
Inspired by the reusability of the modules, we propose PE-MoFormer, a compositional DNN capable of learning a wide range of wireless policies – including but not limited to precoding, coordinated beamforming, power allocation, and channel estimation – with strong generalizability, low sample and space complexity. Simulations demonstrate that the proposed modular PE-based framework outperforms relevant large model in both learning efficiency and inference time, offering a new direction for structured cross-task learning for wireless communications.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Transformer, wireless communications, permutation equivariance, multiple tasks, generalizability

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deep neural networks (DNNs) have been developed for a multitude of wireless problems, such as resource allocation and channel acquisition. Conventional deep learning approaches train a DNN for each specific problem. Despite its good performance, the large number of wireless problems makes it impractical to train and store the specific DNNs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the past several years, large language models (LLMs), with a backbone of Transformer, have attracted widespread attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. These LLMs are pre-trained and then fine-tuned for various downstream tasks. Inspired by their versatility, recent research efforts have been made to leverage pre-trained large models for multiple wireless tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. However, applying large models to wireless communications faces several challenges, say the high sample, memory, and computing demands, the limited adaptability to dynamic wireless systems, and the lack of structured communication knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle the difficulties, a promising direction is resorting to generic wireless knowledge. For example, permutation equivariance (PE) properties, though still underappreciated, exist in a variety of wireless problems. By leveraging PE properties for constraining hypothesis space (i.e., the functions representable by a DNN), DNNs often exhibit lower complexity and better generalizability to dynamic problem sizes (e.g., the number of users)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Nonetheless, how to design a cross-task DNN (say Transformer) by incorporating PE properties is still open.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Related Works</span></h3>

<section id="S1.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>1 </span>Transformers for Wireless Communications</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transformer was originally proposed for sequential tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, whose input and output are sequences of tokens. The core of the Transformer is the attention mechanism, which can capture the dependence among tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Transformer has been introduced to optimize wireless problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, mostly for channel acquisition tasks including channel prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. For example, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, the channels in past time slots were treated as tokens, which are input into a Transformer for channel prediction, using the attention mechanism to capture the temporal correlation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Transformer has also been integrated with other DNNs for resource allocation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. For example, graph Transformers were designed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> to learn baseband and hybrid precoding in multi-user multi-input-single-output (MU-MISO) systems for maximizing spectrum efficiency (SE). Each user was treated as a token, enabling the attention to model multi-user interference (MUI). In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, a Transformer combined with a Kolmogorov-Arnold network was designed to optimize precoding for maximizing the energy efficiency of MU-MISO systems. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, a graph Transformer was designed to jointly optimize bandwidth allocation, precoding at the base station (BS), and coefficients of intelligent reflecting surfaces (IRS).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All these DNNs are task-specific, i.e., each DNN is designed and trained for a single problem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>2 </span>DNNs with Permutation Properties</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A diverse array of wireless optimization problems consist of sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, say signal detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, power/bandwidth allocation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and precoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
As a result, the corresponding <em id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">wireless policies</em>, each of which is a mapping from environment parameters to optimized variables, satisfy permutation properties<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.
There are many types of permutation properties. For example, the MU-MISO precoding policy satisfies an independent two-dimensional (2D)-permutation equivariance (PE) property <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, and the power control for device-to-device (D2D) communications satisfies a joint 2D-PE property <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
The permutation properties of wireless policies can be identified by analyzing the types of sets and their relations in wireless problems with the method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By leveraging the PE properties as prior, the training complexity of the designed DNN can be reduced remarkably. Moreover, a DNN satisfying the PE property induced by a set has the potential for generalizing to the size of the set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. With this size-generalizability, the well-trained DNN can be used for inference in problems of different scales without re-training, which is desirable for dynamic wireless systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For example, both the GNNs designed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> and in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> satisfy the PE property induced by the user set,
which can be generalized to the number of users.
Yet these works only employ the PE property induced by a part of the sets of the considered problems, while the permutability induced by other sets, say the set of antennas (ANs) at the BS, is neglected. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, a unified framework was proposed for designing a graph convolutional network (GCN) whose input-output relation has exactly the same PE property of a wireless policy (i.e., <em id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the GCN has matched PE property to the policy</em>). By exploiting the permutations induced by all sets of a problem, the designed GCNs therein were validated to have lower sample complexity and be generalizable to the sizes of all these sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another example is the tailored Transformers, which have been designed to satisfy simple PE properties. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, a Set-Transformer was designed to satisfy a one-dimensional (1D)-PE property by removing order-sensitive components in Transformers like positional encoding. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, a hierarchical Transformer (Hier-Former) was proposed to satisfy a nested 1D-PE property of a multi-cast policy in MU-MISO systems.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, a 2D-Former and three-dimensional (3D)-Former were designed to satisfy the independent 2D- and 3D-PE properties.
However, these Transformer-based DNNs are unable to satisfy more complex PE properties, say the partial nested 2D-PE property of multi-user multi-input-multi-output
(MU-MIMO) precoding policy. With mismatched property, a DNN has high training complexity or degraded learning performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Again, each of these DNNs was designed to satisfy a single type of PE property, restricting their efficiency for multiple policies with different PE properties.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span>3 </span>DNNs for Multiple Tasks</h4>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since training a specific DNN for every task is costly, several studies strived to design DNNs for more than one wireless task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. For example, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, a DNN was proposed for a couple of wireless tasks with the same input. Specifically, a shared fully-connected neural network (FNN) was designed to extract common representations across tasks, which is cascaded with private FNNs to produce task-specific outputs. This DNN can be applied to problems with different optimization objectives, such as maximizing the sum rate or the minimum rate of users. Nonetheless, as a kind of multi-output learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, the DNN can only be applied to tasks with the same input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Encouraged by the success of LLMs in handling diverse tasks, recent works resorted to large models for multiple wireless problems. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, a pre-trained LLM (GPT-2 or LLAMA2) was fine-tuned to share across three tasks: channel prediction, signal detection, and MU-MISO precoding. For each task, task-specific encoder and decoder were designed before and after the LLM. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, a pre-trained GPT-2 combined with task-specific FNNs or convolutional neural networks was designed for five tasks: channel estimation and prediction, beam selection, and estimation of distance and pathloss.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, a Transformer with over 80M parameters was pre-trained using samples of wireless channels for extracting common channel representations, which is then cascaded with task-specific DNNs to produce outputs for downstream tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the multi-task capability, the upstream or downstream DNNs ought to be designed and trained for every task. Moreover, a large number of training samples (ranging from 14.8k to 50k for each task in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>) and model parameters (ranging from 80M to 7B in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>) are required, which makes pre-training and online fine-tuning challenging.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-B</span> </span><span id="S1.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Motivation and Contributions</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing DNNs for multiple wireless tasks are limited to the tasks with a single input, or suffer from high complexity for pre-training, fine-tuning, and inference. Moreover, the size-generalizability of these DNNs is rarely considered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we challenge the prevailing large model paradigm with structured communication knowledge. In particular, we conceive PE-MoFormer, a DNN with <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PE</span>-aware <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mo</span>dules including Trans<span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">former</span>-style sub-layers, to enable efficient learning across a range of wireless problems. The difficulties in properly designing and assembling modules are addressed by comprehending PE-structured DNNs and wireless tasks.
Our major contributions are as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PE-guided modular design and assembly:</span> We design three modules, each satisfying a specific PE property. By proving that the hypothesis space of a DNN depends on the layer with the largest hypothesis space, we show that these modules and the modules in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> can be assembled into multiple specific PE-Formers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-task compositional DNN:</span> We propose a PE-MoFormer, which enables cross-task learning on diverse wireless tasks by composing appropriate modules to satisfy various PE properties. This design is inspired by the observation that different PE properties can be satisfied by reusing a small set of modules, and the same structural property is shared among more than one wireless policy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High learning efficiency:</span> For a proof-of-concept demonstration, we use simulations to evaluate the proposed PE-MoFormer on the tasks of channel estimation, precoding, coordinated beamforming (CB), and power allocation.
The results show that the PE-MoFormer can achieve good performance across the tasks with only 100 training samples per task and 9.71k parameters in total. Moreover, it has strong generalizability to problem sizes, signal-to-noise ratios (SNRs), and channel statistics, exemplifying its adaptability to dynamic wireless environments.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of this paper is organized as follows: In section <a href="#S2" title="II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, we introduce several PE properties and wireless policies with these properties. In section <a href="#S3" title="III Recaping 1D-Former and 2D-Former ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, we recap two existing Transformer-based DNNs with PE properties. In section <a href="#S4" title="IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>, we analyze the hypothesis space of DNNs, design three modules and assemble them into specific PE-Formers, and propose the cross-task PE-MoFormer. Simulations and conclusions are provided in sections <a href="#S5" title="V Simulations ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> and <a href="#S6" title="VI Conclusion ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notations</span>: <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=[x_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=[x_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{X}_{i,j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{X}_{i,j}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a matrix with the element or sub-matrix in the <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column being <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th column vector of matrix <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{x}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{x}_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{x}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{x}_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a diagonal matrix with the <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th diagonal block being <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix where the subscript <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> may be omitted for brevity. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Tr}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖳𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Tr}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the trace of a matrix. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)^{\mathsf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)^{\mathsf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdot)^{\mathsf{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝖧</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdot)^{\mathsf{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the transpose and conjugate transpose operations, respectively. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> represent the Kronecker product and Hadamard product of a matrix, respectively. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the real and complex number fields, and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Re}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Re}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Im}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖨𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Im}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represent the real and imaginary parts of a complex vector or matrix, respectively. <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subsetneq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊊</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subsetneq</annotation></semantics></math> stands for proper inclusion between sets.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PE Properties and Wireless Policies</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce several PE properties that have been found in wireless communications and present several wireless policies to demonstrate the PE structured knowledge.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The functions defined on sets have PE properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span>1 </span>Sets</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">normal set</span> is composed of unordered elements. A <span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">nested set</span> is a set of subsets, where the elements in every subset and the subsets can be permuted arbitrarily. For example, <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal set where the elements <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> can be arbitrarily permuted. <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\},\{b_{1},b_{2}\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{a_{1},a_{2},a_{3}\},\{b_{1},b_{2}\}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nested set with two subsets, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) can be arbitrarily permuted, and the two subsets can be swapped. However, permutation among the elements in different subsets (e.g., permuting <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) is not allowed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span>2 </span>PE Properties</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function may be defined over more than one set. Depending on the types of sets and the relation between the sets, the functions on sets exhibit different PE properties. A large number of wireless policies are defined on sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, whose inputs and outputs are feature vectors/matrices/tensors of the sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. For easy understanding with simpler notations, we only present eight PE properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A function defined on <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a single set</em> satisfies one of two PE properties as follows, depending on the type of the set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="4"><span id="S2.E1.2a.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1D-PE property:</span></td></tr><tr id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="4"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E1.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\Pi}=f(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚷</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚷</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\Pi}=f(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1a)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nested 1D-PE property:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1.2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\Omega}=f(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Omega})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\Omega}=f(\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Omega}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E1.x1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.x1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\Omega}=(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{sub}}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{\Pi}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\Omega}=(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{sub}}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{\Pi}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary permutation matrix,<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              By arbitrary, we mean <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N!</annotation></semantics></math> possible values for a set with <math id="footnote1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> elements. For instance, a function defined on a normal set with two elements, <math id="footnote1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}\triangleq[\mathbf{y}_{1},\mathbf{y}_{2}]=f([\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2}])\triangleq f(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}\triangleq[\mathbf{y}_{1},\mathbf{y}_{2}]=f([\mathbf{x}_{1},\mathbf{x}_{2}])\triangleq f(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, has two possible permutation matrices, where <math id="footnote1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are the feature matrices of the set. One possible permutation matrix is <math id="footnote1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}=\begin{bmatrix}0,1\\
1,0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}=\begin{bmatrix}0,1\\
1,0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>, which permutes the order of the elements in <math id="footnote1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="footnote1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{x}_{2},\mathbf{x}_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{x}_{2},\mathbf{x}_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and the order of the elements in <math id="footnote1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="footnote1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{y}_{2},\mathbf{y}_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{y}_{2},\mathbf{y}_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The other is <math id="footnote1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}=\mathbf{I}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}=\mathbf{I}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is a nested permutation matrix comprises two levels of permutation (i.e., permuting the elements in each subset with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{i},i=1,\cdots,N_{\mathsf{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{i},i=1,\cdots,N_{\mathsf{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and permuting the subsets with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and
<math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of subsets in the nested set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When a function is defined on <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a normal set</em>,
it satisfies the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">1D-PE property</em> in (<a href="#S2.E1.1" title="In 1 ‣ II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>).
When a function is defined on <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a nested set</em>,
it satisfies the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nested 1D-PE property</em> in (<a href="#S2.E1.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A function defined on <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">two sets</em> satisfies one of six 2D-PE properties as follows, depending on the types of the sets and the relation between the sets.</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ind. 2D-PE property:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2a)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Joint 2D-PE property:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2.2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}^{\mathsf{T}}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}^{\mathsf{T}}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}),\text{ if }\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}=\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}^{\mathsf{T}}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}^{\mathsf{T}}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}),\text{ if }\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}=\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Partial-nested 2D-PE property:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2.3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝛀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2c)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nested 2D-PE properties:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nested ind. 2D-PE property</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2.4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2d)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nested partial-joint 2D-PE property</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2.5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}),\text{ if }\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A,sub}}=\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B,sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}),\text{ if }\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{A,sub}}=\mathbf{\mathbf{\Pi}}_{\mathsf{B,sub}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2e)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nested joint 2D-PE property</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2.6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}),\text{ if }\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}=\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}=f(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}),\text{ if }\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{A}}=\mathbf{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathsf{B}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2f)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">where the two nested permutation matrices are</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{A}}=(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A,sub}}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A},1},\cdots,\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A},N_{\mathsf{sub}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖠</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖠</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{A}}=(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A,sub}}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A},1},\cdots,\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A},N_{\mathsf{sub}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{B}}=(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B,sub}}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B},1},\cdots,\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B},N_{\mathsf{sub}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>diag</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖡</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖡</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{B}}=(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B,sub}}\otimes\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathsf{sub}}})\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B},1},\cdots,\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B},N_{\mathsf{sub}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">When a function is defined on <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">two normal sets</em>, it satisfies the property in (<a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>) or (<a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>).
If the permutations of the two sets are independent (i.e., the elements in each set can be permuted disregarding the permutation of the elements in another set), the function satisfies the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p15.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independent (ind.) 2D-PE property</em>.
If the elements in a set should be permuted together with the elements in the other set (i.e., permuting jointly), the function satisfies the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p15.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">joint 2D-PE property</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">When a function is defined on <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">a normal set and a nested set</em>, it satisfies a <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p16.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partial-nested 2D-PE property</em> in (<a href="#S2.E2.3" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2c</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">When a function is defined on two nested sets, it satisfies the property in (<a href="#S2.E2.4" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2d</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E2.5" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2e</span></a>), or (<a href="#S2.E2.6" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2f</span></a>). If the permutation of a nested set is independent of the other, the function satisfies the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nested ind. 2D-PE property</em>. If the subsets in the two nested sets should be permuted jointly, but the elements in each subset of a nested set can be permuted independently from the permutation of the elements in another nested set, the function has the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p17.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nested partial-joint 2D-PE property</em>.
If both the subsets and the elements in each subset should be permuted jointly, the function has the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p17.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nested joint 2D-PE property</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, a function defined on more than two sets satisfies different high-dimensional PE properties, again depending on the types of the sets and the relation
among the sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">For example, a function defined on three normal sets has the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ind. 3D-PE property</em> as follows, if the permutation in each set is independent of the permutations of the other sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p20.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p20.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ind. 3D-PE property:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{Y}_{\pi_{\mathsf{A}}(i),\pi_{\mathsf{B}}(j),\pi_{\mathsf{C}}(k)}=f(\boldsymbol{X}_{\pi_{\mathsf{A}}(i),\pi_{\mathsf{B}}(j),\pi_{\mathsf{C}}(k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒀</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝖢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑿</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>𝖢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{Y}_{\pi_{\mathsf{A}}(i),\pi_{\mathsf{B}}(j),\pi_{\mathsf{C}}(k)}=f(\boldsymbol{X}_{\pi_{\mathsf{A}}(i),\pi_{\mathsf{B}}(j),\pi_{\mathsf{C}}(k)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p20.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are tensors with three dimensions, the slice along each dimension is the feature of each set, and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the permutation operation on a set.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Some wireless policies have permutation invariance (PI) properties, which are not analyzed for conciseness.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid presenting multiple optimization problems, we provide an optimization problem for a relatively general system setting, which can degenerate into different problems.
Specifically, consider a system with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> cells that share the same spectrum. In the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th cell (denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cell}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Cell</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cell}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>), a BS (denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>) serves multiple user equipments (UEs). Each BS is equipped with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> antennas (ANs) and serves <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> UEs, where each UE is equipped with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> ANs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The precoding matrices at all BSs can be jointly optimized, say to maximize SE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, from the following problem</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{P1}:\max_{\{\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}\}}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\log_{2}\mathrm{det}\Bigg(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{H}_{m,m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{H}_{m,m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P1</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>det</mi><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖧</mi></msup><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖧</mi></msup><mi>𝐇</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{P1}:\max_{\{\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}\}}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\log_{2}\mathrm{det}\Bigg(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{H}_{m,m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{H}_{m,m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigg(\sum_{(i,j)\neq(m,k)}\mathbf{H}_{i,m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{V}_{i_{j}}\mathbf{V}_{i_{j}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{H}_{i,m_{k}}+\sigma_{0}^{2}\mathbf{I}\bigg)^{-1}\Bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="4.000em">OPEN</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mi>𝖧</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>𝖧</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigg(\sum_{(i,j)\neq(m,k)}\mathbf{H}_{i,m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{V}_{i_{j}}\mathbf{V}_{i_{j}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{H}_{i,m_{k}}+\sigma_{0}^{2}\mathbf{I}\bigg)^{-1}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E4Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{s.t.}\quad\quad\quad\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathsf{Tr}(\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}})\leq P_{t},\quad\quad\forall m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:3.167em;" width="3.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝖳𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>𝖧</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{s.t.}\quad\quad\quad\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathsf{Tr}(\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}^{\mathsf{H}}\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}})\leq P_{t},\quad\quad\forall m</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{m_{k}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{i,m_{k}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{i,m_{k}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are respectively the precoding matrix for the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cell}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Cell</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cell}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the channel matrix from <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise power, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the total power of each BS.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the precoding policy as <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=g(\mathbf{\mathbf{H}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=g(\mathbf{\mathbf{H}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{V}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{M})\in\mathbb{C}^{MN_{\mathsf{t}}\times MKN_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{V}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{M})\in\mathbb{C}^{MN_{\mathsf{t}}\times MKN_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{m}=[\mathbf{V}_{m_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{m_{K}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times KN_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{m}=[\mathbf{V}_{m_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{m_{K}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times KN_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the precoding matrix at <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{H}_{i,j_{k}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{MN_{\mathsf{t}}\times MKN_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{H}_{i,j_{k}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{MN_{\mathsf{t}}\times MKN_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the channel matrix from all BSs to all UEs.
By using the method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, it is not hard to examine that the policy is defined on a set containing <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MN_{\mathsf{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MN_{\mathsf{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> ANs at all BSs (called <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set for short) and another set containing <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MKN_{\mathsf{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MKN_{\mathsf{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> ANs at all UEs (called <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set for short), from the dimensions of <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\mathbf{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\mathbf{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set can either be normal or nested sets, and their relation can either be dependent or independent, relying on the system settings. In the sequel, we consider several special cases of Problem <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{P1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P1</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{P1}</annotation></semantics></math> to explain the PE properties induced by the types of sets and their relations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>MU-MISO Precoding (Ind. 2D-PE Property)</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a single cell system where the BS serves <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> single-antenna UEs (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1,N_{\mathsf{r}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1,N_{\mathsf{r}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), Problem <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{P1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P1</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{P1}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to optimizing precoding in a MU-MISO system, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ II-B1 MU-MISO Precoding (Ind. 2D-PE Property) ‣ II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this system setting, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{v}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{v}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times K},\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{h}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{h}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{v}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{v}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times K},\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{h}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{h}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{k}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{k}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{k}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{k}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are respectively the precoding and channel vectors of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th UE (denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th AN at the BS as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}_{n}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>n</mi><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}_{n}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote the AN at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the ANs in the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set or the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set (reducing to a UE set because each UE has a single AN in this setting) can be arbitrarily permuted, the problem consists of two normal sets as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set: {<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}_{1}^{\tt BS},\cdots,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}_{1}^{\tt BS},\cdots,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math>},</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set: {<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}},\cdots,\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}},\cdots,\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>} (i.e.,
{<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{1},\cdots,\text{UE}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{1},\cdots,\text{UE}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>}).
<br class="ltx_break">The MU-MISO precoding policy satisfies the ind. 2D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> reflect the permutations of the ANs at the BS and UEs, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08614v1/MISO.svg" id="S2.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:100/83;" width="100" height="83"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">MU-MISO precoding, <math id="S2.F1.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}=3,K=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}=3,K=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.08614v1/MIMO.png" id="S2.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:100/83;" width="100" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">MU-MIMO precoding, <math id="S2.F1.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}=3,K=2,N_{\mathsf{r}}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}=3,K=2,N_{\mathsf{r}}=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.08614v1/PC.png" id="S2.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:100/83;" width="100" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Power control, <math id="S2.F1.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.08614v1/CB.png" id="S2.F1.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:100/83;" width="100" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">CB and multi-cell power allocation, <math id="S2.F1.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}=2,M=2,K=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}=2,M=2,K=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Several wireless systems. Boxes indicate the subsets in a nested set. In (c) and (d), solid and dashed lines mean the desired and interference channels.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>2 </span>MU-MIMO Precoding (Partial-nested 2D-PE Property)</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a single cell system (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1</annotation></semantics></math>) where the BS serves <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> multi-antenna UEs, Problem <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{P1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P1</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{P1}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to optimizing precoding in a MU-MIMO system, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1.sf2" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ II-B1 MU-MISO Precoding (Ind. 2D-PE Property) ‣ II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this system setting, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{H}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{H}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times KN_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}=[\mathbf{H}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{H}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times KN_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{k}=[\mathbf{h}_{k_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{h}_{k_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{k}=[\mathbf{h}_{k_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{h}_{k_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the channel matrix of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{k_{i}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{k_{i}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the channel vector of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th antenna of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}_{i}^{\mathtt{UE}_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}_{i}^{\mathtt{UE}_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{V}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times KN_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=[\mathbf{V}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{K}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times KN_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{k}=[\mathbf{v}_{k_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{v}_{k_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{k}=[\mathbf{v}_{k_{1}},\cdots,\mathbf{v}_{k_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}}]\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}\times N_{\mathsf{r}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the precoding matrix of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By examining with the method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set is a normal set and the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set is a nested set as follows,</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set: {<math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}_{1}^{\tt BS},\cdots,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mn>1</mn><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}_{1}^{\tt BS},\cdots,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math>},</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}}\!,\ldots\!,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}}\},\ldots\!,\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{UE}_{K}}\!,\ldots\!,\!\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}^{\mathtt{UE}_{K}}\}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}}\!,\ldots\!,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}}\},\ldots\!,\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{UE}_{K}}\!,\ldots\!,\!\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{r}}}^{\mathtt{UE}_{K}}\}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the MU-MIMO precoding policy has the partial-nested 2D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E2.3" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2c</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>3 </span>Power Control (Joint 2D-PE Property)</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When considering <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> cells where each BS equipped with a single antenna serves only a single-antenna UE (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}=1,N_{\mathsf{r}}=1,K=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}=1,N_{\mathsf{r}}=1,K=1</annotation></semantics></math>), Problem <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{P1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P1</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{P1}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to a power control problem (say for D2D communications), as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1.sf3" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ II-B1 MU-MISO Precoding (Ind. 2D-PE Property) ‣ II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}=[h_{i,j}]\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}=[h_{i,j}]\in\mathbb{C}^{M\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired channel from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the UE in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Cell</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (denoted as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i,j}\,\,(i\neq j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i,j}\,\,(i\neq j)</annotation></semantics></math> is the interference channel from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=\mathrm{diag}(v_{1},\cdots,v_{M})\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=\mathrm{diag}(v_{1},\cdots,v_{M})\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the transmit power of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the AN at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{BS}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{BS}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set (i.e., the BS set) and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set (i.e., the UE set) are constituted as,</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set: {<math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{BS}_{1}},\cdots,\text{AN}^{\mathtt{BS}_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{BS}_{1}},\cdots,\text{AN}^{\mathtt{BS}_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>}, (i.e., {<math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{1},\cdots,\text{BS}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{1},\cdots,\text{BS}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>}),</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set: {<math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}},\cdots,\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1}},\cdots,\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>} (i.e., {<math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{1},\cdots,\text{UE}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{1},\cdots,\text{UE}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>}).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The ANs in the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set should be permuted jointly. Thereby, the power control policy is with the joint 2D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>4 </span>CB and Power Allocation (Nested 2D-PE Properties)</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When considering <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> cells where each BS with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> antennas serves <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> single-antenna UEs (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{r}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{r}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>), Problem <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{P1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P1</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{P1}</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates into the optimization of CB in multi-cell MU-MISO system, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1.sf4" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ II-B1 MU-MISO Precoding (Ind. 2D-PE Property) ‣ II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(d)</span></a>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i,j_{k}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i,j_{k}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the channel from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{j_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{j_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the AN at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{j_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{j_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{j_{k}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{j_{k}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By using the method in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, it can be verified that both <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set (i.e., the UE set) are nested sets as follows,</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\!\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{BS}_{1}},\!\cdots\!,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\mathtt{BS}_{1}}\},\!\cdots\!,\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{BS}_{M}},\!\cdots\!,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\mathtt{BS}_{M}}\}\!\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></msubsup><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></msubsup><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\!\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{BS}_{1}},\!\cdots\!,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\mathtt{BS}_{1}}\},\!\cdots\!,\{\text{AN}_{1}^{\mathtt{BS}_{M}},\!\cdots\!,\text{AN}_{N_{\mathsf{t}}}^{\mathtt{BS}_{M}}\}\!\}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set: <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\!\{\text{AN}^{\tt UE_{1_{1}}},\!\cdots\!,\!\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1_{K}}}\},\!\!\cdots\!\!,\{\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{M_{1}}},\!\!\cdots\!,\!\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{M_{K}}}\}\!\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><msub><mn>𝟷</mn><mn>𝟷</mn></msub></msub></msup><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></msub></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.105em;" width="0.105em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.410em;" width="0.410em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msup><mpadded style="width:0.105em;" width="0.105em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub></msup><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\!\{\text{AN}^{\tt UE_{1_{1}}},\!\cdots\!,\!\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{1_{K}}}\},\!\!\cdots\!\!,\{\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{M_{1}}},\!\!\cdots\!,\!\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{M_{K}}}\}\!\}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\!\{\text{UE}_{1_{1}},\!\cdots\!,\!\text{UE}_{1_{K}}\},\!\!\cdots\!\!,\{\text{UE}_{M_{1}},\!\cdots\!,\!\text{UE}_{M_{K}}\}\!\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.105em;" width="0.105em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.410em;" width="0.410em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo>⋯</mo></mpadded><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mpadded style="width:0.330em;" width="0.330em"><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\!\{\text{UE}_{1_{1}},\!\cdots\!,\!\text{UE}_{1_{K}}\},\!\!\cdots\!\!,\{\text{UE}_{M_{1}},\!\cdots\!,\!\text{UE}_{M_{K}}\}\!\}</annotation></semantics></math>),
<br class="ltx_break">and the subsets in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set should be jointly permuted with the subsets in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, the CB policy has the nested partial-joint 2D-property in (<a href="#S2.E2.5" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2e</span></a>), where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathsf{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the permutations of the <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A,sub}}=\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B,sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A,sub}}=\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B,sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> jointly permutes the BS and UEs in each cell, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖠</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A},i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B},i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mrow><mi>𝖡</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B},i}</annotation></semantics></math> permute the ANs at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the UEs in <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Cell</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">When the channel matrix is replaced by an equivalent channel matrix after beamforming,
Problem <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{P1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P1</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{P1}</annotation></semantics></math> degenerates into a multi-cell multi-user power allocation problem. In this setting, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{m}=\mathrm{diag}(v_{m_{1}},\cdots,v_{m_{K}})\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{m}=\mathrm{diag}(v_{m_{1}},\cdots,v_{m_{K}})\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times K}</annotation></semantics></math> is a power allocation matrix at <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the power allocated to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>UE</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This problem also consists of the two nested sets, while both the subsets and the elements in the subsets of the two sets should be jointly permuted. Hence, the power allocation policy satisfies the nested joint 2D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E2.6" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2f</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For SE-maximization MU-MISO precoding, the optimal solution has the following structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{*}=\left(\mathbf{I}+\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}\mathbf{H}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{H}^{\mathsf{H}}\right)^{-1}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{P}^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝖧</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇𝐏</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{*}=\left(\mathbf{I}+\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}\mathbf{H}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{H}^{\mathsf{H}}\right)^{-1}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{P}^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}=\mathrm{diag}(p_{1}/||(\mathbf{I}+\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}\mathbf{H}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{H}^{\mathsf{H}})^{-1}\mathbf{h}_{1}||^{2},\cdots," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝖧</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝐶𝐿𝑂𝑆𝐸</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}=\mathrm{diag}(p_{1}/||(\mathbf{I}+\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}\mathbf{H}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{H}^{\mathsf{H}})^{-1}\mathbf{h}_{1}||^{2},\cdots,</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{K}/||(\mathbf{I}+\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}\mathbf{H}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{H}^{\mathsf{H}})^{-1}\mathbf{h}_{K}||^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">𝑂𝑃𝐸𝑁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>𝖧</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{K}/||(\mathbf{I}+\frac{1}{\sigma_{0}^{2}}\mathbf{H}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\mathbf{H}^{\mathsf{H}})^{-1}\mathbf{h}_{K}||^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Lambda}=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Lambda}=\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as the allocated power to the <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{UE}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">UE</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{UE}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which satisfy <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda_{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}{p_{k}}=P_{t},p_{k}&gt;0,\lambda_{k}&gt;0,\forall k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lambda_{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}{p_{k}}=P_{t},p_{k}&gt;0,\lambda_{k}&gt;0,\forall k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With this structure, one can first learn the mapping from <math id="Thmremark2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmremark2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is 1D-PE to UEs and 1D-PI to ANs, and then use (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Remark 2. ‣ II-B4 CB and Power Allocation (Nested 2D-PE Properties) ‣ II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) to recover the precoding matrix <math id="Thmremark2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a model-driven deep learning has been widely used to optimize the SE-maximization MU-MISO precoding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist wireless policies with more sets. For example,
consider the precoding policy in wideband MU-MISO systems with multiple resource blocks (RBs). In addition to the <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> sets, the RBs also constitute a set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.
Hence, the wideband MU-MISO precoding policy has the ind. 3D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Recaping 1D-Former and 2D-Former</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show how the PE knowledge can be incorporated into Transformer, we recap the 1D- and 2D-Formers designed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> for learning the MU-MISO precoding policy, which satisfy the 1D-PE and ind. 2D-PE properties, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tokens and Representations</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The input of 1D- or 2D-Former consists of multiple tokens, each with an associated representation.
The design of tokens and representations is crucial for the learning performance and size-generalizability of Transformer-based DNNs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For learning the MU-MISO precoding policy, each UE (say UE<sub id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">k</span></sub>) is designed as a token, whose representation is the channel vector of the UE denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(0)}=[\mathsf{Re}(\mathbf{h}_{k}^{\mathsf{T}}),\mathsf{Im}(\mathbf{h}_{k}^{\mathsf{T}})]^{\mathsf{T}}\in\mathbb{R}^{2N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝖨𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(0)}=[\mathsf{Re}(\mathbf{h}_{k}^{\mathsf{T}}),\mathsf{Im}(\mathbf{h}_{k}^{\mathsf{T}})]^{\mathsf{T}}\in\mathbb{R}^{2N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way, the attention mechanism can reflect MUI, thereby improving the learning performance and enabling the generalizability to the number of UEs.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            If each token is designed as a <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math>, then both the 1D- and 2D-Formers perform worse, and both can be generalized to the number of ANs but are not generalizable to the number of UEs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Architecture and Four Modules</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both the 1D- and 2D-Formers are with the encoder-only architecture, which omit the decoder and positional encoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The encoder consists of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers, and each layer is composed of an attention sub-layer (ATT) and a feed-forward network sub-layer (FFN).
In the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer, the representation of the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th token is updated as follows,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{ATT:}\hskip 8.53581pt\mathbf{c}_{k}^{(\ell)}=\sum_{i=1}^{K}\underbrace{\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell-1)\mathsf{T}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)}_{\alpha_{ki}}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{(\ell-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">ATT:</mtext><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mi>𝐔</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>𝖵</mi></msup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{ATT:}\hskip 8.53581pt\mathbf{c}_{k}^{(\ell)}=\sum_{i=1}^{K}\underbrace{\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell-1)\mathsf{T}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)}_{\alpha_{ki}}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{(\ell-1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{FFN:}\hskip 8.53581pt\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell)}=\sigma\Big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{F}}\big(\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell-1)}+\mathbf{c}_{k}^{(\ell)}\big)\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">FFN:</mtext><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖥</mi></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{FFN:}\hskip 8.53581pt\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell)}=\sigma\Big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{F}}\big(\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell-1)}+\mathbf{c}_{k}^{(\ell)}\big)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}^{(\ell)}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell)}N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}^{(\ell)}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell)}N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyper-parameter, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}\!,\!\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell-1)}N_{\mathsf{t}}\times J^{(\ell-1)}N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msup><mpadded style="width:0.275em;" width="0.275em"><mo>,</mo></mpadded><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}\!,\!\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell-1)}N_{\mathsf{t}}\times J^{(\ell-1)}N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{F}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell)}N_{\mathsf{t}}\times J^{(\ell-1)}N_{\mathsf{t}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖥</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{F}}\!\in\!\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell)}N_{\mathsf{t}}\times J^{(\ell-1)}N_{\mathsf{t}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable weight matrices, and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is an activation function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ki}\triangleq\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell-1)\mathsf{T}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ki}\triangleq\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k}^{(\ell-1)\mathsf{T}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math> is the attention score.
When updating <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}^{(\ell-1)}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}^{(\ell-1)}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the attention scores between <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}^{(\ell-1)}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}^{(\ell-1)}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and all token representations <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}^{(\ell-1)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}^{(\ell-1)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,...,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,...,K</annotation></semantics></math>) are computed to capture their dependencies. This mechanism is referred to as <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">global attention</em>, which can reflect MUI.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Parameter-sharing</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The 1D- and 2D-Formers differ in the parameter-sharing in order for satisfying different PE properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the 1D-Former, the weight matrices
are identical (i.e., shared) for all tokens, but these matrices themselves have no structure. In this way, the 1D-Former is equivariant to the permutation of UEs, i.e., satisfying the 1D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E1.1" title="In 1 ‣ II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>). We denote the ATT and FFN with unstructured weight matrices as “<span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ATT</span>”, and “<span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FFN</span>”, respectively. Both are modules with 1D-PE property due to the parameter sharing across tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By further sharing trainable parameters based on the 1D-Former, the 2D-Former satisfies the property in (<a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>). Specifically, all the weight matrices in each layer are designed with identical diagonal blocks and identical off-diagonal blocks.
Take <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}</annotation></semantics></math> as an example, it has the following structure,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}\\
\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}\\
\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{1}\\
\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐔</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖪</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}\\
\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{1}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}\\
\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{2}&amp;\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{1}\\
\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{1}^{\mathsf{K}},\mathbf{U}_{2}^{\mathsf{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell)}\times J^{(\ell)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{1}^{\mathsf{K}},\mathbf{U}_{2}^{\mathsf{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{J^{(\ell)}\times J^{(\ell)}}</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable parameters whose dimensions are independent of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the ATT and FFN whose weight matrices are with the structure in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-C Parameter-sharing ‣ III Recaping 1D-Former and 2D-Former ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) as “<span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ATT (P.S.)</span>”, and “<span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FFN (P.S.)</span>”, respectively, both are modules with the ind. 2D-PE property.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To facilitate the design of cross-task learning, we analyze the hypothesis spaces of DNNs with different PE properties. Then, we design three modules respectively satisfying the properties in (<a href="#S2.E1.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E2.3" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2c</span></a>), and (<a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>). We show that these modules and the four modules in section <a href="#S3" title="III Recaping 1D-Former and 2D-Former ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> can serve as the building blocks, which can be composed into specific PE-Formers for learning the policies in section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-B</span></a>, each ought to be trained for the policy with a particular PE property. Based on the insight from the design rule of specific PE-Formers, we propose a PE-MoFormer by selecting, assembling, and reusing the modules, which is a single DNN pre-trained for learning multiple wireless policies with matched PE properties.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The function family that a DNN (or one of its layers) can represent is called the <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hypothesis space</em> of the DNN (or the layer). For example, the hypothesis space of an FNN is the family of all continuous functions, since FNN can represent any continuous function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> as a family of functions that have a type of PE property.
For example, if the input-output relation of a DNN has the 1D-PE property, then the DNN can only represent the functions with the property, i.e., its hypothesis space is <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}\triangleq\{f_{\Theta}(\cdot)\,|\,f_{\Theta}(\cdot)\text{ has the 1D-PE property}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>1D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> has the 1D-PE property</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}\triangleq\{f_{\Theta}(\cdot)\,|\,f_{\Theta}(\cdot)\text{ has the 1D-PE property}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the trainable parameters in the DNN.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>1 </span>Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From (<a href="#S2.E1.1" title="In 1 ‣ II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E1.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>), we can see that the nested 1D-PE property is a special case of the 1D-PE property. This is because the functions exhibiting the 1D-PE property must satisfy the nested 1D-PE property, due to the fact that the permutation matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1.1" title="In 1 ‣ II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>) includes all possible permutations (including those represented by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>)). However, the functions exhibiting the nested 1D-PE property do not necessarily satisfy the 1D-PE property, since the nested 1D-PE property only ensures the equivariance to the permutation of subsets and the elements in each subset.
Hence, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested 1D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>1D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested 1D-PE</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested 1D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By letting <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}=\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}=\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be seen that the 1D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E1.1" title="In 1 ‣ II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1a</span></a>) is a special case of the ind. 2D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E2.1" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2a</span></a>). This means that the functions satisfying the ind. 2D-PE property must satisfy the 1D-PE property, but the converse statement is not true. Hence, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>1D-PE</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Using similar analyses, the relation among the hypothesis spaces of the DNNs satisfying the PE properties in section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-A</span></a> can be summarized as follows, which is shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A1 Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 3D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}\\
\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested 1D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 3D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>partial-nested 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>1D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested 1D-PE</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 3D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}\\
\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested 1D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 3D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{joint 2D-PE}}\\
\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested partial-joint 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested joint 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 3D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>joint 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested partial-joint 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested joint 2D-PE</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 3D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{joint 2D-PE}}\\
\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested partial-joint 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested joint 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested partial-joint 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested partial-joint 2D-PE</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested partial-joint 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08614v1/hypo.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/208;" width="357" height="208" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Relation of hypothesis spaces of DNNs with different PE properties.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When designing a DNN to learn a function with a type of PE property, appropriately constraining its hypothesis space by exploiting the property can enhance its learning performance and learning efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, when learning a function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with the partial-nested 2D-PE property, an efficient DNN should be designed such that its hypothesis space is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>partial-nested 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the hypothesis space of the DNN (e.g., <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>1D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is <em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">larger</em> than <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>partial-nested 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then more training samples are required to well-approximate the function. With limited samples, the learning performance may degrade due to the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">estimation error</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, which is “distance” between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f_{1}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f_{1}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A1 Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Conversely, if the hypothesis space (e.g., <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is <em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smaller</em> than <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>partial-nested 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{partial-nested 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is not in the hypothesis space, the DNN can only learn a function as close as possible to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{2}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{2}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A1 Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Then, the approximation error (i.e., the “distance” between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{2}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{2}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>) has to be mitigated by enlarging the DNN scale (i.e., using a wider or deeper DNN). Both kinds of errors degrade the performance or the learning efficiency of DNNs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>2 </span>Hypothesis Space of a DNN Consisting of Layers with Different PE Properties</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the layers in a DNN satisfy different PE properties (i.e., have different hypothesis spaces), <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the hypothesis space of the DNN depends on the layer with the largest hypothesis space</em>. Formally, consider a two-layer DNN whose input-output relations of the first and second layers are <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot)\in\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot)\in\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta_{2}}(\cdot)\in\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta_{2}}(\cdot)\in\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are respectively the hypothesis spaces of the two layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> does not necessarily hold. Conversely, if <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{2}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{2}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta_{2}}(g_{\Theta_{1}}(\cdot))\in\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> may not always hold.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Proposition ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We can prove that Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ IV-A2 Hypothesis Space of a DNN Consisting of Layers with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> still holds without the assumption of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) being <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>, by using similar derivations as in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Proposition ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Design of Three Modules</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the analyses in section <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="IV-A1 Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A1</span></a>, we know that when learning policies with joint PE and nested PE properties in (<a href="#S2.E1.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>)-(<a href="#S2.E2.6" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2f</span></a>), the 1D-Former and 2D-Former either requires a large number of training samples for reducing the estimation error or is hard to achieve satisfactory performance due to approximation error.
In order to design DNNs with matched PE properties to such policies, we propose another three modules in the sequel.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>1 </span>Nested Attention Sub-layers</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first design a nested attention sub-layer (NATT) of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer that satisfies the nested 1D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E1.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>), which is a module denoted as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">“NATT”</span>. Then, we design the structure of the weight matrices in the sub-layer, such that the NATT with parameter-sharing can satisfy the partial-nested 2D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E2.3" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2c</span></a>), which is another module denoted as <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">“NATT (P.S.)</span>”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a nested set with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> subsets where each subset consists of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> elements. We design each token as an element in the nested set, and denote the token representation of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th element in the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th subset as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key to designing a module with the nested 1D-PE property lies in distinguishing the tokens within the same subset and across different subsets. If representations of all the tokens in the nested set are updated in the same way (say by using the global attention mechanism as in ATT), the input-output relation of the sub-layer will be equivariant to the permutation of elements not only in the same subset but also across different subsets. Then, the hypothesis space of the sub-layer is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>1D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{1D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is smaller than <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested 1D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>nested 1D-PE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{nested 1D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To differentiate the tokens within the same subset and across different subsets, one can apply an existing approach of designing parameter-sharing for weight matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. However, unlike the ind. 2D-PE property, designing parameter-sharing for satisfying the nested PE property is much more tedious.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Instead, we propose a NATT with both local and global attention mechanisms. The <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local attention</em> only computes the scores of the token representations in the same subset (e.g., <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{k_{i}}^{(\ell-1)},i=1,\cdots,N_{\mathsf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{k_{i}}^{(\ell-1)},i=1,\cdots,N_{\mathsf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>). The global attention computes the scores of all token representations. In this way, the local attention mechanism can distinguish tokens in the same subset from different subsets, and the global attention mechanism can extract the information from different subsets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Specifically, the representation of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th token in the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th subset of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer is updated as follows,</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{NATT:}\hskip 5.69054pt\mathbf{c}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">NATT:</mtext><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{NATT:}\hskip 5.69054pt\mathbf{c}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathsf{s}}}\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell-1){\mathsf{T}}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{d}_{k_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{d}_{k_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}}_{\mathrm{local\,\,attention\,\,}}+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>local</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>attention</mi></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\underbrace{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathsf{s}}}\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell-1){\mathsf{T}}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{d}_{k_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{d}_{k_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}}_{\mathrm{local\,\,attention\,\,}}+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\mathsf{sub}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathsf{s}}}\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell-1){\mathsf{T}}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{d}_{j_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{d}_{j_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}}_{\mathrm{global\,\,attention}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>global</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>attention</mi></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\mathsf{sub}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\mathsf{s}}}\Big(\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(\ell-1){\mathsf{T}}}\big(\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{d}_{j_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}\big)\Big)\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{d}_{j_{i}}^{(\ell-1)}}_{\mathrm{global\,\,attention}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable weight matrices.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The input-output relation of the NATT satisfies the nested 1D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E1.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1b</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Proposition ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{\Pi},\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{\Pi},\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{\Pi},\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚷</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚷</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚷</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚷</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{\Pi},\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}}\mathbf{\Pi},\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{\Pi},\mathbf{\Pi}\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}=\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}\mathbf{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the input-output relation of the </span><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NATT (P.S.)</span><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies the partial-nested 2D-PE property in (<a href="#S2.E2.3" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2c</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">See Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Proof of Proposition ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">If the matrices <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> are with the structure in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-C Parameter-sharing ‣ III Recaping 1D-Former and 2D-Former ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), the four conditions in the proposition can satisfied <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, a Hier-Former was proposed for multi-cast beamforming in MU-MISO systems,
which adopts an encoder-decoder architecture. The encoder learns power allocation, which is recovered to the beamforming matrix by the decoder using a structure similar to (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In Remark 2. ‣ II-B4 CB and Power Allocation (Nested 2D-PE Properties) ‣ II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
However, the Hier-Former can only satisfy the nested 1D-PE property but not the partial-nested 2D-PE property satisfied by the NATT (P.S.).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>2 </span>Diagonal Output Layer</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In (<a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>), the rows and columns of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are permuted with the same matrix. It is easy to show that for any matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and any permutation matrix <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the diagonal elements of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚷</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi}</annotation></semantics></math> are the same as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> but with permuted order.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              For example, let <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=\begin{bmatrix}a&amp;b\\
c&amp;d\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Pi}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Pi}=\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;1\\
1&amp;0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="footnote3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="footnote3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Pi}\mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\Pi}^{\mathsf{T}}=\begin{bmatrix}d&amp;c\\
b&amp;a\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚷</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>d</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>b</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Pi}\mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\Pi}^{\mathsf{T}}=\begin{bmatrix}d&amp;c\\
b&amp;a\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same diagonal elements (i.e., <math id="footnote3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,d</annotation></semantics></math>) with a reversed order.</span></span></span> This is not true for other PE properties such as the ind. 2D-PE property, where the diagonal elements of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> may completely differ.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The observation suggests that the following function extracting the diagonal elements satisfies the joint 2D-PE property,</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{X}),\quad\text{where}\quad y_{i,j}=\begin{cases}x_{i,j},&amp;\text{if }i=j\\
0,&amp;\text{if }i\neq j\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{X}),\quad\text{where}\quad y_{i,j}=\begin{cases}x_{i,j},&amp;\text{if }i=j\\
0,&amp;\text{if }i\neq j\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">It is not hard to prove that
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathsf{T}}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathsf{T}}_{\mathsf{A}}\mathbf{Y}\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}^{\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}=\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖠</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝚷</mi><mi>𝖡</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{A}}=\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that the function <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> possesses the joint 2D-PE property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> lacks trainable parameters and hence cannot be used alone. We can introduce trainable parameters by combining the function with other layers. For example, according to Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ IV-A2 Hypothesis Space of a DNN Consisting of Layers with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, if <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\Theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\Theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the ind. 2D-PE property, then <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(g_{\Theta}(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(g_{\Theta}(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the joint 2D-PE property because <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{joint 2D-PE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>ind. 2D-PE</mtext></msub><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>joint 2D-PE</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{ind. 2D-PE}}\subsetneq\mathcal{F}_{\text{joint 2D-PE}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A1 Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Hence, we can use a DNN that satisfies the ind. 2D-PE property as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\Theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\Theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and use <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as an output layer. Then, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(g_{\Theta}(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(g_{\Theta}(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the property in (<a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>) and is trainable.
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a module that can be used at the output layer<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              Using <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as input layer also ensures the joint 2D-PE property, which however incurs the loss of information in the off-diagonal elements.</span></span></span> of a DNN to satisfy the PE properties with “joint” (say (<a href="#S2.E2.2" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2b</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E2.5" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2e</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E2.6" title="In II-A2 PE Properties ‣ II-A PE Properties of Functions Defined on Different Sets ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2f</span></a>)).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Design of Specific PE-Formers</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the analyses in section <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS2" title="IV-A2 Hypothesis Space of a DNN Consisting of Layers with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A2</span></a>, we can develop a specific PE-Former to match the PE property of a wireless policy by integrating previous modules and carefully designing tokens and their representations. To showcase the practical applicability, we first take the learning of several policies in section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-B</span></a> as examples and then summarize the design rule.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>1 </span>MU-MIMO Precoding (Partial-nested 2D-PE Property)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To allow the attention mechanism for modeling the interference between UEs and data streams, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{k}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\mathtt{UE}_{k}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a token, whose representation is its channel vector, i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(0)}=[\mathsf{Re}(\mathbf{h}_{k_{n}}^{\mathsf{T}}),\mathsf{Im}(\mathbf{h}_{k_{n}}^{\mathsf{T}})]^{\mathsf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝖨𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(0)}=[\mathsf{Re}(\mathbf{h}_{k_{n}}^{\mathsf{T}}),\mathsf{Im}(\mathbf{h}_{k_{n}}^{\mathsf{T}})]^{\mathsf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, similar to the 2D-Former.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A1 Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we know that NATT (P.S.) cascaded by FFN (P.S.) satisfies the partial-nested 2D-PE property. According to Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ IV-A2 Hypothesis Space of a DNN Consisting of Layers with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we can compose the two modules in each layer to learn the MU-MIMO precoding policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer, the token representation is first updated by NATT (P.S.) with <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">(<a href="#S4.E9" title="In IV-B1 Nested Attention Sub-layers ‣ IV-B Design of Three Modules ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>)</span>, where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{s}}=N_{\mathsf{r}},N_{\mathsf{sub}}=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{s}}=N_{\mathsf{r}},N_{\mathsf{sub}}=K</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> are with the structure in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-C Parameter-sharing ‣ III Recaping 1D-Former and 2D-Former ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), which is then input into FFN (P.S.). After updated by <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers, the output <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}^{(L)}_{k_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}^{(L)}_{k_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is normalized as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{k_{n}}=\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(L)}\sqrt{P_{t}/\sum_{i=1}^{K}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\mathsf{r}}}\mathbf{d}_{i_{j}}^{(L)\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{d}_{i_{j}}^{(L)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{k_{n}}=\mathbf{d}_{k_{n}}^{(L)}\sqrt{P_{t}/\sum_{i=1}^{K}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\mathsf{r}}}\mathbf{d}_{i_{j}}^{(L)\mathsf{T}}\mathbf{d}_{i_{j}}^{(L)}}</annotation></semantics></math> to meet the power constraint. In NATT (P.S.), the local attention and global attention respectively reflect the inter-stream interference of a UE and the interference among UEs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>2 </span>CB (Nested Partial-joint 2D-PE Property)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To satisfy the PE property of the CB policy, NATT (P.S.), FFN (P.S.), and the diagonal output layer are required. Both the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set are nested sets in this setting, while using NATT (P.S.) alone can only satisfy the PE property for a single nested set. Yet interference only exists among users but not among antennas, thereby the attention mechanism only needs to be introduced into the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By designing the AN at each UE (say UE<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>) as a token, NATT (P.S.) can satisfy the nested PE property induced by <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt UE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝚄𝙴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt UE}</annotation></semantics></math> set (i.e., the UE set), meanwhile the attention mechanism can reflect MUI and inter-cell interference (ICI). To satisfy the nested PE property induced by the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set, we distinguish <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ANs}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>ANs</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ANs}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> across different subsets via designing different token representations. For each token, we design the channel vector from each BS (say <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{BS}_{m^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>BS</mtext><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{BS}_{m^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>) to UE<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{m_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{m_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a representation, i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}^{(0)}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}=[\mathsf{Re}(\mathbf{h}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}^{\mathsf{T}}),\mathsf{Im}(\mathbf{h}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}^{\mathsf{T}})]^{\mathsf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝖨𝗆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}^{(0)}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}=[\mathsf{Re}(\mathbf{h}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}^{\mathsf{T}}),\mathsf{Im}(\mathbf{h}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}^{\mathsf{T}})]^{\mathsf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since there are <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> BSs, each token is associated with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer, the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-th (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}=1,\cdots,M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}=1,\cdots,M</annotation></semantics></math>) representation of the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-th token is first updated by NATT (P.S.) with (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In IV-B1 Nested Attention Sub-layers ‣ IV-B Design of Three Modules ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{s}}=K,N_{\mathsf{sub}}=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗌𝗎𝖻</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{s}}=K,N_{\mathsf{sub}}=M</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖪</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝖵</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{S}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{K}}_{\mathsf{D}},\mathbf{U}^{\mathsf{V}}_{\mathsf{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> are with the structure in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III-C Parameter-sharing ‣ III Recaping 1D-Former and 2D-Former ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). The representations are then updated by FFN (P.S.).
After <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers, the output <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}^{(L)}=[\mathbf{d}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}^{(L)}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐝</mi><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}^{(L)}=[\mathbf{d}_{m^{\prime},m_{k}}^{(L)}]</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{D}^{(L)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{D}^{(L)})</annotation></semantics></math> by the diagonal output layer. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is then normalized to satisfy the power constraint at each BS. The local and global attention reflect MUI and ICI, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>3 </span>Power Allocation (Nested Joint 2D-PE Property)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The only difference in the PE property of the power allocation policy in section <a href="#S2.SS2.SSS4" title="II-B4 CB and Power Allocation (Nested 2D-PE Properties) ‣ II-B Wireless Policies with Various PE Properties ‣ II PE Properties and Wireless Policies ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II-B4</span></a> from that of the CB policy is that the elements in each subset ought to be permuted jointly instead of independently. We can add additional diagonal output layers on the PE-former for CB to satisfy its property. Specifically, the updated representation after <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers each consisting of NATT (P.S.) and FFN (P.S.), <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}^{(L)}\in\mathbb{R}^{MK\times MK}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}^{(L)}\in\mathbb{R}^{MK\times MK}</annotation></semantics></math>, is first mapped to <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\prime}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{D}^{(L)})\in\mathbb{R}^{MK\times MK}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\prime}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{D}^{(L)})\in\mathbb{R}^{MK\times MK}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}^{\prime}\triangleq\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{V}_{1}^{\prime},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{M}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}^{\prime}\triangleq\mathrm{diag}(\mathbf{V}_{1}^{\prime},\cdots,\mathbf{V}_{M}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{m}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{m}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for the representation of tokens within each subset (say the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th subset), we apply an additional diagonal output layer as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}_{m}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{V}_{m}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐕</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}_{m}=f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\mathbf{V}_{m}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for satisfying the nested joint PE property.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>4 </span>Wideband MISO Precoding (Ind. 3D-PE Property)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For learning this policy, each UE is defined as a token whose representation is its channel vector. The ATT (P.S.) and FFN (P.S.) should be used to satisfy the PE property induced by <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AN}^{\tt BS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>AN</mtext><mi>𝙱𝚂</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AN}^{\tt BS}</annotation></semantics></math> set and UE set. To further satisfy the PE property induced by the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RB</span> set, both modules are shared among RBs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>5 </span>Rule of Module Composition and Token Design</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Specific PE-formers can be designed with the following principle.</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Select modules with appropriate hypothesis spaces according to Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ IV-A2 Hypothesis Space of a DNN Consisting of Layers with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV-A1 Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs with Different PE Properties ‣ IV-A Hypothesis Spaces of DNNs ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Design tokens such that the attention can model the correlation of channels concerned in a task.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Design token representations to distinguish tokens across different subsets when more than one nested sets exist.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We list ten PE-Formers with different PE properties by composing seven modules in different colors in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ IV-C5 Rule of Module Composition and Token Design ‣ IV-C Design of Specific PE-Formers ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>, and summarize the rule for the design as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">1D-PE or nested 1D-PE property</em>: Modules without parameter-sharing are composed. For 1D-PE or nested 1D-PE, ATT or NATT is selected, cascaded by an FFN.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">2D-PE properties without joint permutation</em>: Modules with parameter-sharing are composed (i.e., NATT (P.S.) or ATT (P.S.), and FFN (P.S.)). If there is no nested set, ATT (P.S.) is selected. Otherwise, NATT (P.S.) is selected. If both sets are nested sets, token representations should be designed as for CB.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">2D-PE properties with joint permutation:</em> For joint 2D-PE or nested partial-joint 2D-PE, a diagonal output layer is added. For nested joint 2D-PE, multiple diagonal output layers are required.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ind. and partial-nested 3D-PE properties:</em> The modules adopted for satisfying 2D-PE properties are shared on the third set (e.g., <span id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RB</span> set).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08614v1/comb.svg" id="S4.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/181;" width="429" height="181"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Rule of module composition for specific PE-Formers</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08614v1/uni_PE_Former.svg" id="S4.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/149;" width="381" height="149"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">PE-MoFormer</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Architectures of specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer.
Dashed boxes indicate a layer. For satisfying PE properties induced by two nested sets (marked with “*”), each token has multiple representations.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Design of PE-MoFormer</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ IV-C5 Rule of Module Composition and Token Design ‣ IV-C Design of Specific PE-Formers ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>, different PE properties can be satisfied by reusing several modules. Meanwhile, many wireless policies share common structural properties. Inspired by these observations, we devise a <em id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">PE-MoFormer</em> as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3.sf2" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ IV-C5 Rule of Module Composition and Token Design ‣ IV-C Design of Specific PE-Formers ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(b)</span></a>, which assembles the modules to accommodate diverse PE properties.
During inference, only the selected modules are activated for a given wireless policy, hence the computational complexity is the same as a specific PE-Former.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span>1 </span>Architecture</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PE-MoFormer consists of <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers and a module selector, each layer is composed of several cascaded modules selected from three types of modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One type of modules includes the attention sub-layers, i.e., <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ATT</span>, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ATT (P.S.)</span>, <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NATT</span>, and <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NATT (P.S.)</span>. The second type of modules includes <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FFN</span> and <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FFN (P.S.)</span>. The third type of module is the <span id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">diagonal output layer</span> (i.e., <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖣𝗂𝖺𝗀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{Diag}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>).<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
              For non-PE policies, the positional encoding can be added on the token representations, and then input into layers composed of <span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ATT</span> and <span id="footnote5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FFN</span>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The module selector stores two tables. One stores the PE properties of policies as exemplified in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-D1 Architecture ‣ IV-D Design of PE-MoFormer ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, and the other
stores the module composition rule illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3.sf1" title="In Fig. 3 ‣ IV-C5 Rule of Module Composition and Token Design ‣ IV-C Design of Specific PE-Formers ‣ IV Specific PE-Formers and PE-MoFormer ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
