</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS4.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span>2 </span>Training Strategies</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One training strategy is multi-task learning, where the learning of each wireless policy is treated as a task, and the training set consists of the samples from multiple tasks. The loss function is defined as <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}\lambda_{i}\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i}\lambda_{i}\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for joint training,
where <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the loss and weight of the <math id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th task, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-task learning may lead to conflicts among multiple tasks, degrading the performance of some tasks. An alternative strategy is cross-task learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, where the PE-MoFormer is first pre-trained on some tasks and then fine-tuned for a specific task unseen during pre-training.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5</span></span><span id="Thmremark5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The PE-MoFormer is adaptable to various objective functions. For instance, it can be pre-trained to maximize the sum rate and then fine-tuned to maximize the minimum rate (only with 100 samples according to our simulation).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Simulations</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first evaluate the learning performance and generalizability of specific PE-Formers for several wireless policies to show the impact of the proper composition of designed modules. Then, we evaluate the performance of the PE-MoFormer and compare it with an LLM-based DNN.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Simulation Setup and Hyper-parameters</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The number of ANs at a BS is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{t}}=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗍</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{t}}=64</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of UEs in a cell is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math>, and SNR = <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> dB.
All samples for precoding are generated from the Saleh-Valenzuela (SV) channel model with four clusters and five rays. For wideband MU-MISO precoding, the samples are generated from a tap delay-<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> SV channel model, where the number of taps is set as two, and the number of RBs is set as eight. For MU-MIMO precoding, the number of ANs at each UE is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathsf{r}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>𝗋</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathsf{r}}=4</annotation></semantics></math>. For CB, the number of cells is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3</annotation></semantics></math>, and the ratio of desired channel gains to interference channel gains is randomly selected from <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0.5,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0.5,1)</annotation></semantics></math> since the interference channels are generally weaker than the desired channels.
For multi-cell power allocation, the samples are generated from the equivalent channel after zero-forcing beamforming, where other setups are the same as those for CB. For each task, 1,000 samples are generated for testing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The hyper-parameters of each DNN are listed in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-A Simulation Setup and Hyper-parameters ‣ V Simulations ‣ Modular PE-Structured Learning for Cross-Task Wireless Communications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, and the activation function in hidden layers is <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{tanh(x)}=\frac{e^{x}-e^{-x}}{e^{x}+e^{-x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{tanh(x)}=\frac{e^{x}-e^{-x}}{e^{x}+e^{-x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These setups will be used unless otherwise specified.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Fine-tuned Hyper-parameters</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">DNNs</td>
